<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulaiman Badrul Alam Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1699">1699</a>–<a href="/years/1760">1760</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sulaiman Badrul Alam Shah was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau">Riau</a> from 1722 to 1760. A son of Abdul Jalil IV, he was placed on the throne by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> warriors who drove out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-kecil">Raja Kecil</a> of Siak. In return the Bugis took the new office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-dipertuan-muda">Yang Dipertuan Muda</a>, or viceroy, and held real power, while Riau grew into a thriving port of the Malay world during his reign.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Known as Raja Sulaiman before his accession, he was born on 11 November 1699, the fifth son of Sultan Abdul Jalil Shah IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> by his fourth wife, Che Nusamah. After a rebellion in 1712 he was appointed governor of Pahang, and before the end of 1715 his father named him heir apparent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-kecil">Raja Kecil</a>, who claimed the throne of Johor, deposed and demoted Abdul Jalil in 1718 and ruled until 1722.</p><p>After his father was assassinated in 1721, Raja Sulaiman and his sister Tengku Tengah were taken captive by the Minangkabau forces and made servants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-kecil">Raja Kecil</a>, one to carry his kris and the other his betel box.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> princes expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-kecil">Raja Kecil</a> in 1722, they placed Raja Sulaiman on the throne, and he was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau">Riau</a> on 4 October 1722 as Paduka Sri Sultan Sulaiman Badr ul-‘Alam Shah Khalifat ul-Muminim, the 14th Sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> and Pahang and their dependencies. He had little real influence over the government. The Bugis left him nominal supremacy but kept real power in the office of Yamtuan Muda, which they held themselves, and their dominance went unchallenged for decades. The sultan even wrote to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> asking to be rescued from them, without result.</p><p>Impressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> military strength and by their help against the Minangkabau in the war with the Siak Sultanate, he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in August 1755 and granted the Dutch a monopoly over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>-mining districts of Klang, Linggi and Selangor. These had been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> strongholds, and the grant angered the Bugis chiefs. Sulaiman died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau">Riau</a> on 20 August 1760 and was buried at Batangan. He had seventeen sons and ten daughters, and his second son, Abdul Jalil Muazzam Shah, succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sulaiman Badrul Alam Shah of Johor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau">Riau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=0.54,101.45&amp;year=1738" aria-label="Riau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Sulaiman Badrul Alam Shah"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5973 4954h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5973 4954h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=0.54,101.45&amp;year=1738">What was here in 1738? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
